# Phone as Webcam App - Product Requirements Document

## Executive Summary

Build a cross-platform application that enables Android phones to function as wireless webcams for Mac computers. The system consists of two components: an Android mobile app that captures and streams video, and a Mac desktop app that receives the stream and presents it as a virtual webcam device.

## Product Overview

### Core Value Proposition
Transform any Android phone into a high-quality wireless webcam for Mac, eliminating the need for dedicated webcam hardware and leveraging superior phone camera quality.

### Target Users
- Remote workers and professionals conducting video calls
- Content creators and streamers
- Anyone needing better video quality than built-in Mac webcams
- Users wanting flexible camera positioning

## Technical Architecture

### System Components

#### 1. Android Mobile App
- **Purpose**: Capture video from phone camera and stream to Mac
- **Technology Stack**: Native Android (Kotlin/Java)
- **Key Responsibilities**:
  - Camera capture and encoding
  - Network streaming
  - Connection management
  - Settings UI

#### 2. Mac Desktop App
- **Purpose**: Receive video stream and create virtual webcam device
- **Technology Stack**: Native macOS (Swift)
- **Key Responsibilities**:
  - Network discovery and connection
  - Video decoding and processing
  - Virtual camera driver installation
  - System integration

### Connection Architecture
- **Discovery**: Local network discovery using mDNS/Bonjour + USB device detection
- **Transport**: 
  - WiFi: TCP/UDP for video streaming
  - USB: ADB (Android Debug Bridge) for direct device communication
- **Encoding**: H.264/H.265 for efficient streaming
- **Latency Target**: 
  - USB: <30ms end-to-end
  - WiFi: <100ms end-to-end

## Core Features (MVP)

### 1. Device Discovery & Pairing

#### Android App
- Auto-discover Mac devices on same WiFi network
- Display list of available Mac computers
- Show connection status (connected/disconnected)
- Manual IP address entry option

#### Mac App
- Auto-discover Android devices on network
- Display list of available phones
- Show connection status with visual indicator
- Accept/reject connection requests

### 2. Video Streaming

#### Android App
- Camera selection (front/rear)
- Resolution options:
  - 720p (1280x720)
  - 1080p (1920x1080) - **Default**
  - 4K (3840x2160) - if supported
- Frame rate selection: 15fps, 30fps, 60fps (**60fps default**)
- Auto-focus and exposure control
- Orientation lock (portrait/landscape)

#### Mac App
- Display live video preview
- Video quality settings sync with phone
- Rotation controls (0°, 90°, 180°, 270°)
- Mirror/flip options

### 3. Virtual Camera Integration & OBS Support

#### Mac Implementation
- Create virtual camera device visible to all apps
- Device name: "[Your App Name] Camera"
- Compatibility with:
  - **OBS Studio** (Primary use case)
  - Zoom
  - Google Meet
  - Microsoft Teams
  - Slack
  - FaceTime
  - Any app using standard macOS camera APIs

#### OBS-Specific Optimizations
- **High-quality video source**: Minimal compression for best quality in OBS
- **Low-latency mode**: Optimized for live streaming scenarios
- **Color space support**: Full RGB color space for accurate colors
- **Resolution consistency**: No automatic downscaling - OBS handles scaling
- **Frame timing**: Consistent frame delivery for smooth streaming
- **Audio sync**: Proper timestamp synchronization (future audio feature)

#### OBS Integration Features
- Appears as standard Video Capture Device in OBS
- Supports OBS Studio filters and effects
- Compatible with OBS virtual camera output
- No performance overhead when used with OBS
- Maintains 60fps for smooth streaming

### 4. Network Management

#### Connection Types
- **USB Wired (Primary)**: Zero latency, unlimited bandwidth, stable connection
  - Uses ADB (Android Debug Bridge) protocol
  - Automatic detection when phone plugged in
  - No network configuration needed
  - Highest quality streaming without compression artifacts
- **WiFi (Alternative)**: Wireless convenience with good quality
  - Low latency when on good network
  - Requires same WiFi network
- **Mobile Hotspot**: Direct connection without router

#### Connection Features
- Automatic USB detection and prioritization
- Automatic reconnection on network issues
- Connection quality indicator (USB/WiFi/Signal strength)
- Bandwidth usage monitoring
- Fallback from WiFi to USB prompt if quality degrades
- Seamless switching between connection types

### 5. User Interface

#### Android App
- **Home Screen**:
  - Large "Connect" button
  - List of discovered Mac devices
  - Connection status banner
  - Settings access
- **Camera View (Connected)**:
  - Live camera preview
  - Camera flip button
  - Resolution selector
  - Disconnect button
  - Settings gear icon
- **Settings**:
  - Resolution presets
  - Frame rate selection
  - Video quality/bitrate
  - Audio streaming toggle (future)
  - Keep screen on toggle
  - About/Help

#### Mac App
- **Menu Bar Integration**:
  - Quick access from menu bar icon
  - Connection status indicator
  - Quick disconnect
- **Main Window**:
  - Video preview
  - Device selection dropdown
  - Connection status
  - Settings access
  - Start/Stop virtual camera
- **Settings**:
  - Default resolution
  - Video adjustments (brightness, contrast)
  - Startup options
  - About/Help

## Technical Requirements

### Android App Requirements

#### Minimum Specifications
- Android 8.0 (API Level 26) or higher
- Camera2 API support
- WiFi connectivity
- Minimum 2GB RAM

#### Permissions Required
- Camera
- Internet/Network access (for WiFi mode)
- USB debugging (for wired mode)
- Wake lock (keep screen on)

#### Key Libraries/APIs
- Camera2 API for camera access
- MediaCodec for H.264 encoding
- Network Service Discovery (NSD) for mDNS (WiFi mode)
- Socket programming for streaming (WiFi mode)
- USB accessory APIs for wired connection

### Mac App Requirements

#### Minimum Specifications
- macOS 11.0 (Big Sur) or higher
- Apple Silicon or Intel processors
- 4GB RAM minimum

#### Permissions Required
- Camera (to be provided to other apps)
- Network access
- System Extension (for virtual camera driver)

#### Key Technologies
- CoreMediaIO framework for virtual camera
- AVFoundation for video processing
- VideoToolbox for decoding
- Bonjour for service discovery (WiFi mode)
- IOKit/USB frameworks for USB device communication
- ADB integration for wired connection

### Performance Requirements

- **Latency**: 
  - USB: Maximum 30ms (target 15ms) - Critical for OBS streaming
  - WiFi: Maximum 100ms (target 50ms)
- **Frame Rate**: Solid 60fps at 1080p (no dropped frames)
- **CPU Usage**: 
  - Android: <30% during streaming
  - Mac: <15% during reception (important for OBS performance)
- **Battery**: 
  - USB: No battery drain (phone charges while connected)
  - WiFi: <20% drain per hour
- **Network**: 
  - WiFi: Minimum 10 Mbps for 1080p/60fps
  - USB: No bandwidth limitations

### OBS Performance Targets
- No additional encoding latency in OBS pipeline
- Supports OBS recording at full 60fps
- Compatible with OBS streaming at full bitrate
- Minimal CPU overhead to leave resources for OBS encoding

## Security & Privacy

### Security Measures
- Local network only (no cloud/internet routing)
- Optional PIN code pairing
- Encrypted communication (TLS)
- Automatic device timeout after inactivity
- No video recording or storage by default

### Privacy Features
- Clear indicator when camera is active
- One-tap disconnect on phone
- Notification when streaming starts
- Explicit permission requirements

## User Flows

### First-Time Setup Flow

1. **Mac App**:
   - Download and install Mac app
   - Grant system extension permission
   - Grant network permission
   - Launch app from menu bar

2. **Android App**:
   - Install from Google Play Store
   - Grant camera and network permissions
   - Connect to same WiFi as Mac

3. **Pairing**:
   - Open Android app
   - See Mac listed in available devices
   - Tap to connect
   - Mac app shows confirmation
   - Connection established

### Regular Usage Flow

**USB Connection (Recommended for OBS):**
1. Connect Android phone to Mac via USB cable
2. Enable USB debugging on phone (one-time setup)
3. Mac app auto-detects phone connection
4. Open Android app (or auto-launches)
5. Camera stream starts automatically via USB
6. Open OBS Studio on Mac
7. Add "Video Capture Device" source
8. Select "[Your App Name] Camera"
9. Phone camera feed appears in OBS at 1080p/60fps
10. Configure and start streaming/recording

**WiFi Connection (Wireless convenience):**
1. Ensure phone and Mac on same WiFi
2. Open Android app
3. Tap connected Mac device
4. Camera stream starts automatically
5. Open OBS and select camera source

## Success Metrics

### Key Performance Indicators
- Connection success rate: >98% (USB should be near 100%)
- Average latency: 
  - USB: <20ms
  - WiFi: <75ms
- Frame drops: <0.1% at 1080p/60fps
- App crash rate: <0.1%
- User rating: >4.5 stars
- OBS compatibility: 100%

### User Engagement Metrics
- Daily active users
- Average session duration
- Reconnection frequency
- Feature usage statistics

